 square (90 deg) density = 1.57080  (= pi/2 = 1.57080); hexagon WINS.

================================================================================
 THE FINITE CLASSIFICATION (Reinhardt 1918 + Rao 2017) makes the covering search FINITE
================================================================================
 Convex polygons that monohedrally tile the plane: ALL triangles, ALL quadrilaterals,
 exactly 3 hexagon types, exactly 15 pentagon types; NONE with >= 7 sides. (Rao 2017 closed it.)
 For a LATTICE covering the Voronoi cell is CENTRALLY SYMMETRIC + convex with <=6 sides
  => only the HEXAGON or the RECTANGLE (parallelogram). The angle scan above: hexagon (60deg,
  rhombus=2 equilateral triangles) beats rectangle/square (90deg, 2 right triangles).
  So among lattice coverings the optimum is the EQUILATERAL-TRIANGULAR (hexagonal) lattice -- a
  FINITE check (hexagon vs rectangle), won by the hexagon (Kershner). The triangular lattice = the
  A2 'god': the 3-fold (tridiagonal/A2) structure, p6m, the zeta_6-line's ambient lattice (HYP-3715).

================================================================================
 APERIODIC (Socolar-Taylor) does NOT beat it
================================================================================
 Fejes Toth: the hexagonal lattice is the thinnest covering among ALL coverings (not just lattices).
 The Socolar-Taylor aperiodic monotile is a DECORATED HEXAGON (forces aperiodicity) -- still in the
 hexagonal family; aperiodicity gives no covering-density gain. So the optimum stays hexagonal/A2.
